speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, I rise on a question of privilege.
In view of the statements made in the house yesterday, and in view of the fact that I have already made a voluntary statement to the R.C.M.P., I wish to state as follows:
1.	It was as a member of parliament that I made representations in the case of one Mr. Rivard. In the course of those representations I am convinced that I did not exercise or attempt to exercise any undue influence, nor did I do anything reprehensible;
2.	In view of that statement given to the R.C.M.P., I am sure that my name is mentioned in the report of the R.C.M.P.
If a judicial inquiry is held in this matter, as was inferred yesterday, I request that I be granted every opportunity of appearing before the investigating commissioner;
3.	I wish to state and insist upon the fact that, right or wrong, I never thought that I should inform the Prime Minister about this matter, and it was only this morning that I told him about it;
4.	Until an inquiry has established the whole truth about this matter, and until a decision is rendered, I am asking the Prime Minister to relieve me of my duties as parliamentary secretary.

I just wanted to make a correction in Hansard in my remarks of last night. I refer to page 10587, the sentence which reads:
-we should have accepted some kind of defence from the Minister of Finance;
What I said was, "we should have expected some kind of defence from the Minister of Finance of Mr. Chevrier." I suggest this really needs to be made quite clear because the main point of my peech was that Mr. Chevrier was the one who was bearing the brunt of some quite nasty interpretations. I felt that if the Minister of Finance was going to make a defence of the government he should at least have defended his former colleague.

Supply-Legislation
ress. A number of other important and fundamental matters are currently before the committee with which it would, of course, be improper for me to deal at this time.
Although the committee has already produced 14 reports, incorporating more than 30 recommendations, some hon. members may feel we have been rather slow in bringing before the house proposals which could be described as far reaching. But I should like to emphasize that from the very outset the committee has taken the view that changes in our procedure should be evolutionary rather than revolutionary; that they should be acceptable in all parts of the house; that they should be achieved through the slow process of discussion and persuasion to the point of unanimous conviction rather than by a majority forcing its will. Every member of the committee has taken this view, with the result that we have achieved a collective team spirit and a common awareness of the problems which need to be solved. This surely is the way to success.
Perhaps I may be permitted to enumerate a few of the procedural changes which have already been implemented on the recommendation of the committee:
-a revised procedure for the question period;
-a safety-valve in what the hon. member for Kootenay West has described as "the late show" for debating grievances on the adjournment motion at the end of the day;
-an improved procedure for dealing with notices of motions for the production of papers;
-the better regulation of statements on motions;
-the limitation of debate on the resolution stage of a money bill and the tidying up of the committee stage procedure;
-the definition of the powers of the Chair in relation to the acceptance of emergency adjournment motions;
-certain reforms affecting the appointment and sittings of committees;
-provision in the standing orders for the house not to sit on certain statutory holidays.
All of these reforms, I am convinced, have contributed and will continue to contribute to the more effective functioning of the parliamentary machine.
